During Blackout Periods, directors, executives, and designated employees are prohibited from:

- Purchasing or selling Company Securities;
- Making gifts or charitable donations of Company Securities;
- Making elections, changes, investment decisions, or re-allocations in any 401(k) plan, pension plan, deferred compensation plan, or other benefit or deferral plans that involve Company Securities;
- Effecting cashless exercises of options.

These restrictions are in place to ensure compliance with insider trading laws and to prevent unfair advantage. The blackout periods are designed to support the principle that Covered Persons must not trade in Company Securities during times when material non-public information (MNPI) may exist.

The trading restrictions are enforced during Blackout Periods, which typically begin at the close of business on the 15th day of the last month of each fiscal quarter or 30 days prior to the scheduled release of financial results, whichever is earlier, and end when financial results are released to the public.

These restrictions apply regardless of whether MNPI is possessed, and Covered Persons must not trade in Company Securities when they are in possession of MNPI, even outside of a Blackout Period.

